{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of Butson's theorem (1962): if a Butson-type Hadamard matrix H(p, n) exists, with p prime and n at least 2, then p divides n. A Butson-type Hadamard matrix H(k, n), for k > 0, is an n by n complex matrix whose entries are k-th roots of unity and whose rows are mutually orthogonal, M M* = n I; H(2, n) are the real Hadamard matrices. The statement and definition are those of Formal Conjectures (ButsonHadamardMatrix.lean), copied verbatim. The proof is the standard vanishing-sum argument: two orthogonal rows give a vanishing sum of n p-th roots of unity; counting how often each power of exp(2 pi i / p) occurs gives a rational polynomial of degree below p vanishing at that root, so the p-th cyclotomic polynomial 1 + X + ... + X^(p-1) divides it, every power occurs equally often, and p divides n.",
  "authors": [
